# Building and installing Coin

This guide builds Coin from source, installs it to a user-writable prefix, and
verifies the installation with a separate C++ project.

## Prerequisites

- Git, CMake 3.21 or newer for the commands below, and a C/C++ toolchain with
  C++11 support. CMake can generate Makefiles, Ninja files, Xcode projects, or
  Visual Studio projects.
- OpenGL development libraries. For a Linux build with GLX and EGL, install the
  development headers and libraries for OpenGL, X11, and EGL. On macOS, install
  the Xcode Command Line Tools. On Windows, install Visual Studio with the
  **Desktop development with C++** workload.
- Doxygen is needed when building the API documentation. Boost is not required.

Clone the repository and run the remaining commands from its root:

```sh
git clone --recurse-submodules https://github.com/coin3d/coin.git
cd coin
```

A source archive can also be used for a library-only build. The Awesome
documentation and CPack packaging use submodules supplied by the recursive
clone.

## Build and install

### Linux and macOS

Use a single-configuration generator such as Unix Makefiles or Ninja. The
example installs under the checkout so it needs no administrator permissions.

```sh
prefix="$PWD/coin-install"
cmake -S . -B coin-build -DCMAKE_BUILD_TYPE=Release \
  -DCMAKE_INSTALL_PREFIX="$prefix" -DCOIN_BUILD_TESTS=OFF
cmake --build coin-build --parallel 8
cmake --install coin-build --config Release
```

On macOS this installs Coin as a library. If you specifically need the
`Inventor` framework, configure a shared build with
`-DCOIN_BUILD_MAC_FRAMEWORK=ON`. For a GLX build on macOS, install XQuartz and
configure with `-DCOIN_BUILD_MAC_X11=ON`. SoXt also requires Motif.

### Windows

In PowerShell, use the Visual Studio 2022 generator and select the architecture
you will use for both Coin and its consumers.

```powershell
$prefix = Join-Path (Get-Location) "coin-install"
cmake -S . -B coin-build -G "Visual Studio 17 2022" -A x64 "-DCMAKE_INSTALL_PREFIX=$prefix" -DCOIN_BUILD_TESTS=OFF
cmake --build coin-build --config Release --parallel
cmake --install coin-build --config Release
```

## Build a program against the installed package

The [standalone CMake example](examples/cmake-consumer/README.md) uses
`find_package(Coin CONFIG REQUIRED)` and links to the exported `Coin::Coin`
target. It initializes Coin and prints the installed version. It needs no GUI
binding or display server.

### Linux and macOS

In the same shell, with `prefix` set to your installation directory:

```sh
cmake -S examples/cmake-consumer -B consumer-build \
  -DCMAKE_BUILD_TYPE=Release -DCMAKE_PREFIX_PATH="$prefix"
cmake --build consumer-build --config Release
./consumer-build/coin-version
```

### Windows

In the same PowerShell session:

```powershell
cmake -S examples/cmake-consumer -B consumer-build -G "Visual Studio 17 2022" -A x64 "-DCMAKE_PREFIX_PATH=$prefix"
cmake --build consumer-build --config Release
$env:PATH = "$(Join-Path $prefix bin);$env:PATH"
.\consumer-build\Release\coin-version.exe
```

The program prints a line such as `SIM Coin 4.0.10`. If several Coin
installations are available, check that `Coin_DIR` in
`consumer-build/CMakeCache.txt` points inside the intended prefix. To select the
package explicitly, set `Coin_DIR` to the directory containing
`coin-config.cmake`. Use a fresh consumer build directory when switching
installations.

Next, see the [examples index](examples/README.md) to open a scene with
[Quarter](https://github.com/coin3d/quarter), the Qt integration for Coin. For
existing applications, see the [SoQt](https://github.com/coin3d/soqt),
[SoWin](https://github.com/coin3d/sowin), or
[SoXt](https://github.com/coin3d/soxt) build instructions. Point each binding's
`CMAKE_PREFIX_PATH` at the Coin installation and any toolkit dependencies.

## Optional build features

| CMake option | Purpose |
| --- | --- |
| `COIN_BUILD_SHARED_LIBS` | Builds a shared library by default. Set to `OFF` for a static build. |
| `COIN_BUILD_TESTS` | Builds the test suite by default. Run `ctest --test-dir coin-build -C Release --output-on-failure` after building it. |
| `COIN_BUILD_EXAMPLES` | Adds the examples and their dependencies when enabled. Defaults to `OFF`. |
| `COIN_BUILD_DOCUMENTATION` | Enables the classic `documentation` target. Defaults to `OFF`. |
| `COIN_BUILD_AWESOME_DOCUMENTATION` | Enables the `documentation_awesome` target. Defaults to `OFF`. |
| `COIN_BUILD_DOCUMENTATION_MAN`, `COIN_BUILD_DOCUMENTATION_QTHELP`, `COIN_BUILD_DOCUMENTATION_CHM` | Request man pages, Qt Help, or Windows HTML Help with the classic documentation build. Qt Help and Windows HTML Help need their corresponding tools. |
| `COIN_BUILD_INTERNAL_DOCUMENTATION` | Enables internal API documentation in the classic build. |
| `COIN_BUILD_GLX`, `COIN_BUILD_EGL` | Select the corresponding OpenGL integrations where available. |
| `COIN_BUILD_MAC_FRAMEWORK`, `COIN_BUILD_MAC_X11` | Select a framework or X11 integration on macOS. Both default to `OFF`. |
| `HAVE_MULTIPLE_VERSION` | Uses versioned paths for headers and documentation. |
| `COIN_BUILD_SINGLE_LIB` | Controls how the build groups sources into library targets. Defaults to `ON` on Windows and `OFF` elsewhere. |
| `COIN_BUILD_MSVC_MP` | Enables parallel compilation with MSVC. |
| `COIN_USE_CPACK` | Enables packaging from the `cpack.d` submodule. Defaults to `OFF`. |

See the [Doxygen build guide](docs/legacy/doxygen.md) for documentation targets
and validation.

To create packages, configure with `-DCOIN_USE_CPACK=ON`, build Coin, and run
`cpack --config <configuration>.cmake` from `coin-build/cpack.d` using one of
the configurations generated for your platform. Examples include
`debian.cmake`, `fedora.cmake`, `windows.cmake`, and `darwin-dmg.cmake`.
Windows NSIS packages require [NSIS](https://nsis.sourceforge.io/Download).

## Troubleshooting

- Use a fresh build directory if changing generators, compilers, or
  architectures. With a single-configuration generator, separate build
  directories make it easy to keep Debug and Release builds.
- Separate multiple dependency prefixes in `CMAKE_PREFIX_PATH` with semicolons
  and quote the complete argument.
- On Windows, use matching architectures and compatible MSVC runtimes across
  Coin and the consumer. If the program loads the wrong DLL, check which
  installation's `bin` directory comes first on `PATH`.
- When asking for help, include the Coin revision, operating system, CMake and
  compiler versions, configure command, and first complete error message. Search
  the [issue tracker](https://github.com/coin3d/coin/issues) and the
  [coin3d-discuss archive](https://groups.google.com/group/coin3d-discuss) for
  related reports.
